"[2601.16161] On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs (https://borowiecki.dev/abs/2601.16161)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last week; Cite as:  | arXiv:2601.16161 [math-ph]\n\n# Mathematical Physics\n\n[Submitted on 22 Jan 2026 (v1), last revised 20 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs\n\nAuthors:Tim Heib, David Edward Bruschi\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs, by Tim Heib and David Edward Bruschi\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:We present a method for associating labeled directed graphs to finite-dimensional Lie algebras that are $graph$-$admissible$. The proposed framework enables visual identification of key structural algebraic features. We first formalize this approach and establish several classes of Lie algebras that admit the categorization as graph-admissible Lie algebras, and then investigate suitable valid graphs for this approach by translating the antisymmetry of the Lie bracket and the Jacobi identity into rules that such graphs must satisfy. Furthermore, based on these foundations, we provide graph-theoretic criteria for solvability, nilpotency, presence of ideals, simplicity, semisimplicity, and reductiveness of an algebra. We also present practical algorithms for constructing such graphs and those associated with the lower central series and derived series via iterative pruning procedures. We further cover specific examples, such as the Schrödinger and Lorentz algebras, which illustrate the applicability of the developed tools to physically relevant cases. Moreover, we explore applications in physics, where the method facilitates computation of similarity transformations essential for determining quantum mechanical time evolution via the Lie-algebraic factorization method. We also discuss a possible generalization of the framework based on magma-gradings of Lie algebras and formulate related open questions. Our approach bridges algebraic and combinatorial perspectives, offering theoretical tools for this area of mathematical physics.\n\nComments:  | We have significantly overhauled the manuscript. In particular, we have withdrawn Conjectures 80, 82, and 83 of the original version. The appendix has also been updated. 95 pages (66+29), 32 figures (24+8)\nSubjects:  | Mathematical Physics (math-ph); Quantum Physics (quant-ph)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2601.16161 [math-ph]\n   | (or arXiv:2601.16161v2 [math-ph] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.16161 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Tim Heib [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 22 Jan 2026 18:09:16 UTC (4,722 KB)\n[v2] Tue, 20 Oct 2026 11:05:03 UTC (1,436 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.16161)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{heib2026structuralpropertiesliealgebras, title={On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs}, author={Tim Heib and David Edward Bruschi}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.16161}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math-ph}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.16161}, }\nSubmitted 22 Jan 2026\n\n# On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs\n\nForschungszentrum J sulich Universit sat des Saarlandes\n\nTH\n\nTim Heib\n\nDE\n\nDavid Edward Bruschi\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a method for associating labeled directed graphs to finite-dimensional Lie algebras, thereby enabling rapid identification of key structural algebraic features. To formalize this approach, we introduce the concept of graph-admissible Lie algebras and analyze properties of valid graphs given the antisymmetry property of the Lie bracket as well as the Jacobi identity. Based on these foundations, we develop graph-theoretic criteria for solvability, nilpotency, presence of ideals, simplicity, semisimplicity, and reductiveness of an algebra. Practical algorithms are provided for constructing such graphs and those associated with the lower central series and derived series via an iterative pruning procedure. This visual framework allows for an intuitive understanding of Lie algebraic structures that goes beyond purely visual advantages, since it enables a simpler and swifter grasping of the algebras of interest beyond computational-heavy approaches. Examples, which include the Schrödinger and Lorentz algebra, illustrate the applicability of these tools to physically relevant cases. We further explore applications in physics, where the method facilitates computation of similtude relations essential for determining quantum mechanical time evolution via the Lie algebraic factorization method. Extensions to graded Lie algebras and related conjectures are discussed. Our approach bridges algebraic and combinatorial perspectives, offering both theoretical insights and computational tools into this area of mathematical physics.\n\nView Paper\n\n\n## Discussion\n\nComment\n\n9 views\n\nv1\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{heib2026structuralpropertiesliealgebras, title={On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs}, author={Tim Heib and David Edward Bruschi}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.16161}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math-ph}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.16161}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the maximal subgroups of almost simple and primitive perfect groups | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.19443)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; @misc{capilla2026maximalsubgroupssimpleprimitive, title={On the maximal subgroups of almost simple and primitive perfect groups}, author={Patricia Medina Capilla and Luca Sabatini}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.19443}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19443}, }\nSubmitted 18 Jun 2026\n\n# On the maximal subgroups of almost simple and primitive perfect groups\n\nPM\n\nPatricia Medina Capilla\n\nLS\n\nLuca Sabatini\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that, if $G$G is a finite almost simple group and $H$H is a maximal subgroup of $G$G, then the $10$10 th term of the derived series of $H$H is perfect. The same is true if $G$G is perfect and $H$H is core-free. The constant $10$10 is best possible.\n\nCite\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{capilla2026maximalsubgroupssimpleprimitive, title={On the maximal subgroups of almost simple and primitive perfect groups}, author={Patricia Medina Capilla and Luca Sabatini}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.19443}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19443}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/400003106_On_the_structural_properties_of_Lie_algebras_via_associated_labeled_directed_graphs)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2601.16161\nPreprint\n\n# On the structural properties of Lie algebras via associated labeled directed graphs\n\n  * January 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2601.16161\n\nAuthors:\n\nTim Heib\n\nTim Heib\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nDavid Edward Bruschi\n\n  * Forschungszentrum Jülich\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\